WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.850
We all love Jurassic Park, but nothing's perfect.

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:07.620
Yes, it's unreasonable to expect director
Steven Spielberg and writers Michael Crichton

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.310
and David Koepp to know everything about dinosaurs
and prehistoric eras.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:17.320
But the film still committed a number of easily
avoidable sins, and glossed over stuff that

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.359
should have been explained more thoroughly.

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:24.439
Here's a look at some of the dumb things about
Jurassic Park that everyone just ignored.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:26.230
Mosquito-oh-no

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:31.819
In the movie, park owner John Hammond's introductory
video shows viewers how Jurassic Park is even

00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:36.960
possible: a super-scientific process that
includes the extraction of dinosaur blood

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.769
from mosquitoes preserved in amber.

00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:40.170
"Oh, Mr. DNA!

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.489
Where did you come from?"

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:44.190
"From your blood!"

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.920
In the movie, scientists stick the needle
into the amber, extract the dinosaur blood,

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.809
and then, well, you know the rest.

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:56.730
But in real life, extracting dino DNA from
a 60 million-year-old mosquito isn't actually

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:57.970
possible.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:02.039
Although the amber does preserve the mosquito
nicely, time would still take its toll and

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:03.340
the tissue would decay.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:08.189
But let's pause for a second and imagine that,
sure, you can just clone a big ol' dinosaur

00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:09.750
from a few drops of blood.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.360
So why is Hammond wasting his time and fortune
cloning gigantic killer reptiles?

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:20.290
He could be saving lives and actively improving
the world by making new kidneys, or livers,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.869
or hearts or—

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:24.130
"Hold onto your butts."

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.010
—right, or even butts!

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.159
Fraudulent fossils

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:33.479
Jurassic Park's mosquitoes were unearthed
near the Dominican Republic, but in reality,

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:37.930
it simply doesn't produce fossils that old
— the oldest amber discovered in the region

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.090
only dates back 15 million years.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.539
Sorry, Spielberg, you've still got nearly
50 million years to go before getting dinosaur

00:01:44.539 --> 00:01:45.539
blood.

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:50.320
Now, if you want a wonderful vacation spot,
rich with coconut drinks, cocoa beans, creepy

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.579
parrots, and awesome baseball players — well,
the Dominican Republic is the place for you,

00:01:55.579 --> 00:01:56.579
man!

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:57.579
Problem plants

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:02.840
Meanwhile, the movie’s paleobotanist Ellie
Sattler observes and makes notes about Cretaceous

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.899
plant life that somehow flourishes in the
park.

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:07.899
But how did the plants even get there?

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:11.980
We know scientists were able to recreate dinosaurs
using the mosquito, but how were they able

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.700
to reproduce and replicate extinct plants?

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.200
Last time we checked, mosquitos don’t eat
no salad.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.069
"You don't win friends with salad.

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:20.810
You don't win friends with salad.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.650
You don't win friends with salad.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.430
You don't win friends with salad.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:25.940
You don't win friends with salad."

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.870
Besides, we all know that messing around with
cloning can yield unpredictable results.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.980
"That's chaos theory."

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.200
Chances are pretty good that if the dinosaurs
hadn't gotten all aggro and murdery, the plants

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.200
would've.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.709
"Feed me!"

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:42.150
No leg to stand on

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:47.090
Jurassic Park features scenes in which brachiosaurs
lean back on their hind legs to reach higher

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.510
branches containing leaves — which are poisonous,
by the way — for them to chew on.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.209
It looks pretty cool, right?

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:54.760
Great bit of movie magic happening.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.379
Of course, those dinosaurs weren't actually
able to stand on their hind legs: they weren't

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.670
built to support themselves that way.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.069
Oh, well.

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:08.030
Anyway, everyone knows dinosaurs had other,
way more exciting ways of reaching higher

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.440
branches.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.849
Why can't these movies ever be accurate, man?

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:19.040
What the frog?

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.120
In the movie, the dinosaur DNA that's extracted
from fossilized mosquitoes is buffed up and

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.560
completed with frog DNA to fill in the missing
sequences.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:33.730
"We use the complete DNA of a frog to fill
in the holes and complete the code!"

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:37.900
This raises a number of critical issues, arguably
the most important of which is the fact that

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:42.290
filling in missing genomes with cross-species
DNA isn't really feasible at all.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.230
But even accounting for suspension of disbelief,
there are a number of closer relatives readily

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.830
available, like birds or crocodiles.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:54.159
And given the choice between a frog or a crocodile,
which do you think would be more likely to

00:03:54.159 --> 00:04:05.489
do a good dinosaur impression?

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:07.180
Too much poop

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.890
The movie shows Dr. Ian Malcolm stumbling
upon a sick triceratops' heaping mound of

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:13.890
doodle.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.470
"That is one big pile of s---."

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.221
While that has now become an internet meme,
it's also a pretty accurate statement: although

00:04:19.221 --> 00:04:23.480
we don't know much about triceratops poop,
given the size of tyrannosaurus droppings

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.260
archaeologists have found, it's likely that
the giant pile of dino doo seen in the movie

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.460
is way, way too big.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.720
A real dinosaur wouldn't have been able to
poop out such a gigantic pile -- even after

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:43.860
a bunch of ice cream.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.020
Thanks for watching!

